Facebook's proposals create its own  digital currency called Libre has gotten  a lot of backlash from global regulators  Facebook has burned down the house over  and over it's been the subject of acidic  u.s. congressional hearings and Frances  finance minister went so far as to say  that it was an assault on national  sovereignty do officials in other  countries including China Germany and  Italy have expressed similar concerns  here's what regulators are worried about  for one lawmakers worried that Libre has  the potential to disrupt the monetary  system if Libre becomes the currency for  Facebook's 2.4 billion or so users to  say nothing of other companies that  might accept it it could become a  globally significant currency how Libre  is going to be what's called in crypto  markets a stable coin it will be a  pegged currency that maintains basically  a 1 to 1 value with the US dollar that's  a different model than Bitcoin Bitcoin  trades freely and its value is  determined solely upon what people are  willing to pay for it to maintain that  peg the group that it will be governing  this currency called the Libre  Association will maintain a reserve for  every dollars worth of Libre that is  created one dollar is going to be put  into the reserve theoretically that  means that this reserve could hold  billions or even trillions of dollars  worth of currencies and short term  securities that is a very powerful tool  in capital markets if that money got  moved around significantly from one  currency to another it could absolutely  upset global markets now that's not to  say that Facebook or the Liebherr  Association is expected to do something  like weaponize these reserves however  this is the first time that not a  government but a private company or a  group of private companies would have  that kind of power Facebook and the  Liebherr association of companies  backing the project say they are  committed to working with authorities to  achieve a safe transparent and  consumer-friendly implementation of the  digital currency lawmakers who are  already looking into Facebook for a  number of other reasons are not  comfortable with Facebook's power the  tech company is battling scrutiny on  multiple fronts from its role in  elections to privacy to even antitrust  investigation  Facebook is dangerous they don't respect  the power of the technologies Facebook's  business model it's rolling spreading  propaganda and its controversial efforts  to rein it in have infuriated lawmakers  we will have to make very strong  commitments so that people trust us and  we will have to honor those commitments  for a very long period of time to earn  people's trust at the core of a lot of  the distrust among governments is that  Facebook has been criticized for its  handling of user data before and Libra  would create a traceable record of all  its users transactions just imagine a  company being able to combine your  identity your online habits and your  spending patterns that is an  unprecedented ocean of data at this  summer's congressional hearings Facebook  executive David Marcus said the company  and the Liebherr Association will keep  the spending data separate from the user  identities lawmakers course were  skeptical of that I have serious  concerns with Facebook's plans  regulators scrutiny is one of the  reasons libras viability has been called  into question and some key companies  that initially back the project have  ducked out with Visa saying its decision  to work with the project in the future  depends on libras ability to satisfy  regulatory expectations we'll have to  wait and see whether this scrutiny from  regulators makes Leever stronger or ends  up threatening its existence altogether
 

